{
  "summary": "The proposal's three-tier extraction strategy and test-first sequencing are sound in principle, but the plan underestimates three practical risks. First, the characterization test suite is treated as a prerequisite but no concrete scope is defined—Textual's async rendering, reactive attribute side effects, and modal lifecycle make snapshot-based testing brittle, and the effort to build a reliable harness could easily exceed the refactoring effort itself; a more pragmatic gate would be a smaller set of smoke tests covering the 5-6 most critical user flows (create feature, move phase, reject, edit, refresh) rather than attempting comprehensive screen snapshots. Second, the 'free async functions accepting PipelineApp' pattern proposed for Tier 2 is not just an aesthetic concern about God Objects—it creates a real maintenance trap where every extracted function's implicit dependency on PipelineApp's 30+ reactive attributes means that any change to PipelineApp's internal state shape silently breaks all action modules with no compile-time or import-time signal, making the codebase arguably harder to maintain than the monolith since the coupling is now hidden across file boundaries instead of visible in one file. A lightweight mitigation would be to define a small TypedDict or dataclass for each action module's inputs at extraction time, forcing explicit dependency documentation even before a full Protocol/interface layer. Third, the _check_for_changes polling problem (line 2596) is dismissed as a separate concern, but it is deeply entangled with the refactoring: the method calls _load_features, _snapshot, _refresh_kanban_widgets, _push_to_server, and _update_detail_view—five different responsibilities spanning data loading, diffing, rendering, and networking—so any extraction of these subsystems must decide who owns the change-detection loop and how the extracted modules coordinate refresh, making this the architectural linchpin that should be designed first rather than deferred."
}
